  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 210 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nSunday night through Thursday:\n\nSynopsis: A pronounced area of Canadian high pressure will build \ninto the western Great Lakes Sunday night through Monday, bringing \nlight winds and very dry air to southern Wisconsin. The light winds \nwill combine with attendant clear skies to support efficient cooling \nduring the overnight periods Sunday into Monday & Monday into \nTuesday, with areas of frost possible during each time frame. The \nhigh will vacate east of the area on Tuesday, with a weak \ndisturbance and surface front progged to track across the area \nTuesday night into Wednesday. A few showers could develop in the \nvicinity of the passing disturbance & front, with hazards appearing \nunlikely. Dry conditions will prevail through the remainder of the \nperiod behind the departing upper disturbance & surface front.  \n\nTuesday night into Wednesday: The lone (albeit very small) precip \nchances of the period enter ahead of a weak upper disturbance and \ncold front. Precip probabilities from the NBM are currently in the \n~10-20% range, which is a testament to a very dry antecedent air \nmass & marginal frontal convergence. With the values appearing \nreasonable given environmental limitations, have thus maintained NBM \nprecip probabilities in the mid-day update. Modeling points toward \nmarginal CAPE & precipitable water values, suggesting low hazards \npotential in any precip. Will nevertheless monitor trends as this \nportion of the period draws closer. \n\nQuigley",
